WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.840
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Have you ever run

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:03.570
a workload that uses Virtual
Machines in Google Cloud?

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:06.300
And while you're enjoying a cup
of tea during a quick break,

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.970
your alerts are firing, the
service is having issues,

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:11.580
and users are really unhappy?

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:14.670
You rush back to your computer
and log into the Cloud Console.

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:18.180
The VMs are still up, and system
resources are seemingly normal.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.643
What can you do to
troubleshoot from here?

00:00:20.643 --> 00:00:22.560
Well, you might want to
look at a few things--

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.240
whether your AP
processes are running

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.910
on the VMs, how much
resources they're consuming,

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:29.340
and whether there are
any errors in the logs.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.200
But as you may remember
from our previous episode,

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.020
you need to have the
Ops Agent installed,

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:35.760
running, and
collecting and sending

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.630
data to get this kind of
information from your VMs.

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:40.380
If you ever have issues
getting installed,

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:43.000
running, or getting data,
you've come to the right place.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:43.800
My name is Yuri.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.390
I'm an SRE at Google Cloud.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:48.030
And today, I'm going to show
you how to troubleshoot the Ops

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.640
Agent to make sure you always
have the VM observability

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.700
data you need and get back to
that tea before it gets cold.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:57.190
[LIGHT MUSIC]

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:59.350
Let's start at the beginning
with the installation.

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:00.970
There are two
primary reasons why

00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:04.209
you might have trouble
getting the agent installed.

00:01:04.209 --> 00:01:06.040
The first reason is
that you're trying

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:08.650
to install it on an
unsupported operating system.

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:10.630
You'll know that's the
case if you see an error

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:12.820
message like this, where
the installer isn't

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:16.750
able to download the
right package for your OS.

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:19.060
The second reason is a
configuration conflict,

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:22.150
specifically with the old
monitoring or logging agents.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:25.360
In this case, you'll see an
error message like this one.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.820
You can't have both the Ops
Agent and the old agents

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:29.590
installed at the
same time, so you'll

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:32.620
have to uninstall
the old agents first.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:34.330
Once the agent is
installed, you can

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:37.090
confirm that it's running by
using the system CDL status

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:40.510
command, like this, and looking
for the confirmation messages

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:43.510
that the agent components
are started and running.

00:01:43.510 --> 00:01:46.210
If the agent is not running,
you may need to start it.

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:48.940
If it has issues starting,
you may have a conflict

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:50.898
or an invalid configuration.

00:01:50.898 --> 00:01:52.690
Let's have a look at
how to start the agent

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:55.570
and sort out startup issues.

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:57.490
If the agent isn't
running for any reason,

00:01:57.490 --> 00:01:59.530
you'll see a message
like this, saying

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:02.710
that the service isn't running
when you check its status.

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:05.800
Use the Start command to
try to get it running again.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:07.600
You might get a
message like this,

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.539
saying that either the Cloud
Ops Agent itself or one

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:12.550
of its modules failed to start.

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:15.877
The former usually means you
have an invalid configuration.

00:02:15.877 --> 00:02:18.460
Have a look at the documentation
on how to configure the agent

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:19.660
to sort this out.

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:23.400
We'll, of course, link to
it in the episode notes.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.260
If a module can't
start, it's likely

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:27.900
because they can't send
logs or metrics to the API.

00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:30.120
Often, this is caused
by invalid credentials,

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:32.970
such as a disabled service
account or an expired key.

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:34.800
Networking issues
and proxy issues,

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.780
such as an invalid
proxy configuration,

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.700
can also cause this.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.500
Once you've verified that the
agent is installed and running,

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:43.920
you should be getting its
metrics and logs adjusted.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.040
If that's not the case,
you'll need to figure out why.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.600
Generally, you'll
be investigating

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:49.990
three types of issues.

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:52.030
The first has to do
with the agent itself,

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:53.790
the second is with
the sources of data

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:55.680
you're trying to
ingest, and the third

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.380
is with sending collected
data to the respective API.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:01.530
Let's have a look at each.

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:03.690
If the issue you're seeing
has to do with logs,

00:03:03.690 --> 00:03:06.540
check to see if the logging
module is running like this.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:08.820
If the output is anything
other than successful,

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:10.350
you may need to
restart the agent

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:12.750
or validate its configuration.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:14.245
If the logging
module is running,

00:03:14.245 --> 00:03:15.870
the next thing to
check is whether it's

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:17.400
able to read the logs.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:19.950
One way to check is to use
the local metric endpoint,

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:21.570
like this.

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:24.270
If everything looks good so
far, but you're still not seeing

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.220
the logs you
expect, check to see

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:28.830
if the agent is getting any
errors from the logging API.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:32.130
You can do that in the
logging module log, like this.

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:34.620
You might see permission
errors if the API isn't enabled

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:37.410
in the project, the service
account has been disabled,

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:38.940
doesn't have the
right permissions,

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.820
the service account key expired,
or if the agent is using

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:45.240
the VMs default service account,
insufficient scopes on the VM

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:46.140
itself.

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:48.670
You might also be out
of quota for the API.

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:52.200
You'll need to resolve these
to resume log ingestion.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.020
We'll use a similar approach to
troubleshoot the matrix module.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:56.280
Let's have a look.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.230
You can check the status
of the matrix module

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:00.450
by looking at module
logs and syslog.

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:02.160
If there aren't any
logs, the service

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:03.730
may not be running properly.

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:05.610
Otherwise, you can check
the logs themselves

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:08.910
to see if there are
any issues noted there.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:10.560
This log is also
where you would check

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.530
for any API errors, such as
Permission Denied or quota

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:14.762
issues.

00:04:14.762 --> 00:04:16.470
At this point, you
should have everything

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:19.019
you need to sort out agent
issues and never be in the dark

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:21.540
about your VMs running
on Google Cloud.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:23.320
Thanks so much for
watching today.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:25.650
Don't forget to like and
subscribe to never miss out

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:28.350
on more "Engineering for
Reliability" with Google Cloud.

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:29.280
See you soon.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.630
[LIGHT MUSIC]

